#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068571 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2a41ede-381e-55f6-99e5-1baf1ea348a1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of a Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/id #uuid "c1dcb0a7-441f-5628-ba23-71d43d9ea96c" :question/math-academy-id "e-495" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int x^2\\cos(3x) \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={x}^{2} & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{2})(\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))-\\int (\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\frac{2}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we use integration by parts to evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int x\\sin(3x) \\textrm{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\text{d}x=-\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x(-\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))-\\int (-\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))+{C}_{1} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{9}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+{C}_{1}\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C_1$ is a constant of integration.\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\frac{2}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\frac{2}{3}(-\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{9}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+{C}_{1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+\\frac{2}{9}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\frac{2}{27}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\frac{2{C}_{1}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}((9{x}^{2}-2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+6x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = -\\dfrac{2C_1}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068572 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98a67346-c2c9-5d22-8adf-479fea81eaa2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of a Logarithmic Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/id #uuid "b81ff533-2067-5d1a-b265-df1ad91fa8da" :question/math-academy-id "e-496" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_1^e 32x^3(\\ln x)^2 \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2} & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=32{x}^{3} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int 32{x}^{3}\\text{d}x=8{x}^{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 32{x}^{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}(8{x}^{4})-\\int (8{x}^{4})(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8{x}^{4}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-16\\int {x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int {x^3\\ln(x)} \\textrm{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{3} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int {x}^{3}\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4})-\\int (\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4})(\\frac{1}{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}\\int {x}^{3}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{16}+{C}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C_1$ is an integral constant. So, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 32{x}^{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\text{d}x & =8{x}^{4}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-16\\int {x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8{x}^{4}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-16(\\frac{{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{16}+{C}_{1}) \\\\  & =8{x}^{4}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-4{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+{x}^{4}-16{C}_{1} \\\\  & ={x}^{4}(8{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1)+C\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = -4C_1.$ Finally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}32{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\text{d}x & ={{x}^{4}(8{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1)|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =[(e)^{4}(8{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e))}^{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)+1)]-[(1)^{4}(8{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1))}^{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+1)] \\\\  & =[{e}^{4}(8-4+1)]-1 \\\\  & =5{e}^{4}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068636 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b69f026a-50b3-505d-bd9d-8b295691b66e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of an Exponential Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/id #uuid "8ec71af3-4534-5ef1-8745-3220102ba838" :question/math-academy-id "e-7053" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_0^1 (t^2+1)e^{2t+1} \\, \\textrm{d} t.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={t}^{2}+1\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=2t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{2t+1}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =({t}^{2}+1)(\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{2})(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}}{2}-\\int t\\,{e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int t\\,e^{2t+1}\\,\\textrm{d}t.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{2t+1}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int t\\,{e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t)(\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{2})(1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{t\\,{e}^{2t+1}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{t\\,{e}^{2t+1}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{4}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}}{2}-\\int t\\,{e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}}{2}-(\\frac{t\\,{e}^{2t+1}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{4}+D) \\\\  & =\\frac{({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}}{2}-\\frac{t\\,{e}^{2t+1}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{4}-D \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{4}(2{t}^{2}-2t+3)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = -{D}.$ \n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t & ={[\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{4}(2{t}^{2}-2t+3)]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{e}^{2(1)+1}}{4}(2(1)^{2}-2(1)+3))-(\\frac{{e}^{2(0)+1}}{4}(2(0)^{2}-2(0)+3)) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{4}(2-2+3)-\\frac{e}{4}(0-0+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{3}}{4}-\\frac{3e}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{3}-3e}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546984 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2a41ede-381e-55f6-99e5-1baf1ea348a1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of a Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "052b33f0-0879-5289-a4d7-39be855e9b3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546258} {:answer/id #uuid "1a1765fc-6178-5a34-819c-f6621cb09c9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{4}+2\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546307} {:answer/id #uuid "4c2900c5-5719-539a-837b-df60e0071e30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{4}-3\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546420} {:answer/id #uuid "5503e4a0-2bb6-52f2-a04d-816373cc0117" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546439} {:answer/id #uuid "95fe2f50-e6ea-5cda-b510-d9b70073028f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-2\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546583}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5503e4a0-2bb6-52f2-a04d-816373cc0117" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546439} :answer-field/id #uuid "af82264f-d4d2-5e87-919b-0a6badfef83f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2f4365a0-c1aa-5cdc-8199-72e9694e40fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-114869" :question/problem "$\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. \n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{2})(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2})(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546981 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2a41ede-381e-55f6-99e5-1baf1ea348a1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of a Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0acbf8b7-a7ec-512d-910a-4a88e103b84a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546269} {:answer/id #uuid "40ad84d0-df22-514c-b7f4-ef327ed396f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546399} {:answer/id #uuid "476132db-4ef6-5515-b111-f7e77ba4eb98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546408} {:answer/id #uuid "99d14bbd-7d91-50b8-8833-f775d7983756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546592} {:answer/id #uuid "b13e628a-6d28-55cc-9835-5ec1309a30de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b13e628a-6d28-55cc-9835-5ec1309a30de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546646} :answer-field/id #uuid "22ec1ca4-4aac-500e-804f-1010088a8fda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "29a95d8e-b457-570f-bf3b-6c486e09dbc5" :question/math-academy-id "q-114874" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={x}^{2}+1\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we use integration by parts to evaluate $\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(1)dx \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2(-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+D) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-2D.$ Finally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={[({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{2}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -(({0}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+2\\cdot 0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡0-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{2}+1)\\cdot 0+2\\pi (-1)-2\\cdot 0-0 \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546978 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98a67346-c2c9-5d22-8adf-479fea81eaa2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of a Logarithmic Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c2709fa-4adc-5605-a11a-8ab65411323d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2e-1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546345} {:answer/id #uuid "b389c4a4-9a5f-5726-baac-fe68902eb839" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e+2}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546652} {:answer/id #uuid "b3ae699e-ca50-5532-b3f9-e8044519c63f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2e+1}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546654} {:answer/id #uuid "de3cc4ab-836f-59ce-b290-e93434f9fd0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e-2}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546743} {:answer/id #uuid "f1417a6b-fec7-5120-8761-55733a3efa2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e-1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546792}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de3cc4ab-836f-59ce-b290-e93434f9fd0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e-2}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546743} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f6244bc-3786-5afc-8ff3-37c064cb0ba6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2441fae2-b285-52be-9809-5b0ff2ea594e" :question/math-academy-id "q-114887" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{e}({log}_{2}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=({log}_{2}⁡x)^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2{log}_{2}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({log}_{2}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={({log}_{2}⁡x)}^{2}(x)-\\int (x)(\\frac{2{log}_{2}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{({log}_{2}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\int {log}_{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate  $\\int {log}_{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{2}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {log}_{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({log}_{2}⁡x)(x)-\\int (x)(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{log}_{2}⁡x-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{log}_{2}⁡x-\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({log}_{2}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =x{({log}_{2}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(x{log}_{2}⁡x-\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+D) \\\\  & =x{({log}_{2}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{2x{log}_{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+\\frac{2x}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{x{ln}^{2}⁡x}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}-\\frac{2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}+\\frac{2x}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}({ln}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-\\frac{2D}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.$\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${log}_{2}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.$\n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}({log}_{2}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{x}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}({ln}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2)]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}({ln}^{2}⁡e-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+2)-\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}({ln}^{2}⁡1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}-\\frac{2}{{ln}^{2}⁡2} \\\\  & =\\frac{e-2}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250319 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98a67346-c2c9-5d22-8adf-479fea81eaa2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of a Logarithmic Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "082f5ffa-db8f-5b86-9f1f-66bec8888277" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247495} {:answer/id #uuid "312a7284-86a2-58d1-925a-aae469b9cc31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}+2}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247869} {:answer/id #uuid "4646f294-569b-5c9a-8e3f-8b4bffc32dd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-2e}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248052} {:answer/id #uuid "8a1ab61f-f876-54ff-ac9a-719572398e93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248654} {:answer/id #uuid "aed8ce21-2fef-51ea-932a-bf3d76234eac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248988}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a1ab61f-f876-54ff-ac9a-719572398e93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248654} :answer-field/id #uuid "90297971-15bc-5633-a065-ce4100255f03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249928}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dca36867-5a6f-51bd-9e64-ad043dde12a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-114900" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{e}4x({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2{log}_{3}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=4x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 4x\\,\\text{d}x=2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4x({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={({log}_{3}⁡x)}^{2}(2{x}^{2})-\\int (2{x}^{2})(\\frac{2{log}_{3}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}-\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\int x{log}_{3}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int x{log}_{3}⁡(x)\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{3}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int x\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{log}_{3}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({log}_{3}⁡x)(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{log}_{3}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{log}_{3}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4x({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{x}^{2}({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}-\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(\\frac{{x}^{2}{log}_{3}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+D) \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}-\\frac{2{x}^{2}{log}_{3}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}{ln}^{2}⁡x}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}-\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2{ln}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-\\frac{4D}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.$ \n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${log}_{3}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.$\n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}4x({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2{ln}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2{ln}^{2}⁡e-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+1)-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2{ln}^{2}⁡1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2-2+1)-\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(0-0+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547038 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2a41ede-381e-55f6-99e5-1baf1ea348a1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of a Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "207ce1bb-8bde-56c6-bc5c-bd9959f561ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-{x}^{2}-3x+2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-(3x+2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546321} {:answer/id #uuid "3d5bf71e-d1f7-5404-b06d-cdd7ddc9172e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}+3x-2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-(3x+2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546386} {:answer/id #uuid "6c1d844c-2030-5617-b47c-4ad4758ca496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-{x}^{2}-2x+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+(x+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546491} {:answer/id #uuid "e9b070a4-b26f-53c6-a6cb-ac154bdd8423" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-{x}^{2}-3x+2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+(2x+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546776} {:answer/id #uuid "fd13586a-50c7-5494-a9e4-d9a877da7ffd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}+3x-2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+(2x+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546819}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9b070a4-b26f-53c6-a6cb-ac154bdd8423" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-{x}^{2}-3x+2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+(2x+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546776} :answer-field/id #uuid "113fdcf6-88f3-5444-8636-104a1da82ce5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546836}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a2a087e4-737f-55e1-b912-34c295e29547" :question/math-academy-id "q-12701" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int ({x}^{2}+3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. \n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={x}^{2}+3x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x+3 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({x}^{2}+3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+3x)(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))(2x+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+2\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\text{d}x+3\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+2\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\text{d}x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we use integration by parts to evaluate $\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))(1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({x}^{2}+3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-({x}^{2}+3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+2(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+D)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+C+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\  & =(-{x}^{2}-3x+2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+(2x+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=2D.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547044 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b69f026a-50b3-505d-bd9d-8b295691b66e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of an Exponential Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a5d5539-f19e-58d4-89ba-c26370b32e9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{9}(9{x}^{2}+6x+4)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546378} {:answer/id #uuid "7e65fb00-30d6-59d7-ba38-99e4d7919af5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{9}(3{x}^{2}-15x+1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546530} {:answer/id #uuid "9ba2da88-7afb-5630-ab57-bfbfbca438f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{9}(9{x}^{2}-6x+8)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546599} {:answer/id #uuid "c18beed8-cfd5-5a08-8fa1-a6733a3642d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{27}(9{x}^{2}-7x-2)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546687} {:answer/id #uuid "f39741fc-058e-5d16-9fa8-ead8b1d6a65b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{27}(3{x}^{2}+7x-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546797}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ba2da88-7afb-5630-ab57-bfbfbca438f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{9}(9{x}^{2}-6x+8)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546599} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8ae469c-ade7-5edf-add1-d9b620e0dc4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546944}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b68d5307-5a40-5cd7-a13f-e872bf3c3818" :question/math-academy-id "q-14739" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int (3{x}^{2}+2){e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=3{x}^{2}+2\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{3x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (3{x}^{2}+2){e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2}+2)(\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3})-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3})(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{(3{x}^{2}+2){e}^{3x}}{3}-\\frac{6}{3}\\int x{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{(3{x}^{2}+2){e}^{3x}}{3}-2\\int x{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int x{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{3x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x(\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3})-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3})(1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x{e}^{3x}}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x{e}^{3x}}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{9}+D \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{9}(3x-1)+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (3{x}^{2}+2){e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{(3{x}^{2}+2){e}^{3x}}{3}-2(\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{9}(3x-1)+D) \\\\  & =\\frac{(3{x}^{2}+2){e}^{3x}}{3}-\\frac{2{e}^{3x}}{9}(3x-1)-2D \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{9}(3(3{x}^{2}+2)-2(3x-1))-2D \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{9}(9{x}^{2}-6x+8)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-2D.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547050 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2a41ede-381e-55f6-99e5-1baf1ea348a1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of a Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06628961-0a23-58ec-9114-8a2869d3f184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}+2x+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-(x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546261} {:answer/id #uuid "5e2c30ac-5a3b-59e6-b7ac-40a7a35ce0ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}+3x+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-2(x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546454} {:answer/id #uuid "95cb1031-6660-5c68-b3d1-9694e6dd5cb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+(x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546581} {:answer/id #uuid "a1332706-0cd5-5e89-b7d3-3fbf37bf3786" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-({x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546610} {:answer/id #uuid "b64ee9cc-18f5-56b5-98d2-bbe2e3880df6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}+2x-2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+2(x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546660}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b64ee9cc-18f5-56b5-98d2-bbe2e3880df6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}+2x-2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+2(x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546660} :answer-field/id #uuid "adb0d541-280d-59dc-985d-50704ecec3c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546915}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c07f9c82-279d-54bf-a7cc-232522a75290" :question/math-academy-id "q-3395" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int ({x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. \n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={x}^{2}+2x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x+2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))(2x+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-2\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\text{d}x-2\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-2\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\text{d}x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we use integration by parts to evaluate $\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(x)(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))(1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =({x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-2(-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+D)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+C+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x-2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+2(x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-2D.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320933 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b69f026a-50b3-505d-bd9d-8b295691b66e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of an Exponential Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b075ced-5a58-52b1-9238-36dc27c2ec56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{e}^{4}-3{e}^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319719} {:answer/id #uuid "18f48006-0521-5dbd-8e2a-63bc4720879a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3(e^{4}-e^{2})}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546305}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18f48006-0521-5dbd-8e2a-63bc4720879a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3(e^{4}-e^{2})}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546305} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d64743d-9a58-56a2-86a7-e957db42f825" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320187}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1a2f1472-983a-5988-9015-40ac8b729fa5" :question/math-academy-id "q-351983" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}3{t}^{2}{e}^{2t+2}\\,\\text{d}t=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={t}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=2t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=3{e}^{2t+2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int 3{e}^{2t+2}\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{2t+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3{t}^{2}{e}^{2t+2}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =({t}^{2})(\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{2t+2})-\\int (\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{2t+2})(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}{e}^{2t+2}-3\\int t{e}^{2t+2}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int t{e}^{2t+2}\\,\\text{d}t.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{2t+2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{2t+2}\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int t{e}^{2t+2}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t)(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t+2})-\\int (\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t+2})(1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{2t+2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2t+2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{2t+2}-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{2t+2}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3{t}^{2}{e}^{2t+2}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}{e}^{2t+2}-3\\int t{e}^{2t+2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}{e}^{2t+2}-3(\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{2t+2}-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{2t+2}+D) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}{e}^{2t+2}-\\frac{3}{2}t{e}^{2t+2}+\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{2t+2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-3D.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}3{t}^{2}{e}^{2t+2}\\,\\text{d}t & =(\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}{e}^{2t+2}-\\frac{3}{2}t{e}^{2t+2}+\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{2t+2})\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{2}({1}^{2}){e}^{2(1)+2}-\\frac{3}{2}(1){e}^{2(1)+2}+\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{2(1)+2})-(\\frac{3}{2}({0}^{2}){e}^{2(0)+2}-\\frac{3}{2}(0){e}^{2(0)+2}+\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{2(0)+2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{4}-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{4}-3{e}^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320934 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b69f026a-50b3-505d-bd9d-8b295691b66e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of an Exponential Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "823c5e0e-13c8-50c5-9177-f0541f81db79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11{e}^{3}-7e}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319786} {:answer/id #uuid "97500619-bf65-5acf-b9c2-c7f0aa395ed4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11e^{3}-7e}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546587}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97500619-bf65-5acf-b9c2-c7f0aa395ed4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11e^{3}-7e}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546587} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0269145-0ae9-55e4-a07c-12dcf8ceee64" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e235b03c-6338-5aac-b78a-cee6da78b778" :question/math-academy-id "q-351991" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{2}({y}^{2}+3){e}^{2y-1}\\,\\text{d}y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={y}^{2}+3\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}=2y, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}={e}^{2y-1}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y=\\int {e}^{2y-1}\\,\\text{d}y=\\frac{{e}^{2y-1}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({y}^{2}+3){e}^{2y-1}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =({y}^{2}+3)(\\frac{{e}^{2y-1}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{2y-1}}{2})(2y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{({y}^{2}+3){e}^{2y-1}}{2}-\\int y\\,{e}^{2y-1}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int y\\,{e}^{2y-1}\\,\\text{d}y.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=y\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}={e}^{2y-1}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y=\\int {e}^{2y-1}\\,\\text{d}y=\\frac{{e}^{2y-1}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y\\,{e}^{2y-1}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =(y)(\\frac{{e}^{2y-1}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{2y-1}}{2})(1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{y\\,{e}^{2y-1}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2y-1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{y\\,{e}^{2y-1}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2y-1}}{4}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({y}^{2}+3){e}^{2y-1}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\frac{({y}^{2}+3){e}^{2y-1}}{2}-\\int y\\,{e}^{2y-1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{({y}^{2}+3){e}^{2y-1}}{2}-(\\frac{y\\,{e}^{2y-1}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2y-1}}{4}+D) \\\\  & =\\frac{({y}^{2}+3){e}^{2y-1}}{2}-\\frac{y\\,{e}^{2y-1}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{2y-1}}{4}-D \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2y-1}}{4}(2{y}^{2}-2y+7)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-D.$ \n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}({y}^{2}+3){e}^{2y-1}\\,\\text{d}y & ={[\\frac{{e}^{2y-1}}{4}(2{y}^{2}-2y+7)]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{e}^{2(2)-1}}{4}(2(2)^{2}-2(2)+7))-(\\frac{{e}^{2(1)-1}}{4}(2(1)^{2}-2(1)+7)) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{4}(8-4+7)-\\frac{e}{4}(2-2+7) \\\\  & =\\frac{11{e}^{3}}{4}-\\frac{7e}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{11{e}^{3}-7e}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320935 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b69f026a-50b3-505d-bd9d-8b295691b66e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of an Exponential Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec3dfe4a-5ecf-52b0-bf9f-2434195360cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13{e}^{5}-9e}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320097} {:answer/id #uuid "a0c8d37a-15b0-55b6-8550-bdc73f2f07a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13e^{5}-9e}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0c8d37a-15b0-55b6-8550-bdc73f2f07a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13e^{5}-9e}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546609} :answer-field/id #uuid "a761614e-3f16-5f4f-81d2-802676a5bea1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320497}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ddd7fba2-ad87-5331-9a58-1b0e4fa091f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-351994" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2}+4){e}^{2y+1}\\,\\text{d}y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={y}^{2}+4\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}=2y, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}={e}^{2y+1}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y=\\int {e}^{2y+1}\\,\\text{d}y=\\frac{{e}^{2y+1}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({y}^{2}+4){e}^{2y+1}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =({y}^{2}+4)(\\frac{{e}^{2y+1}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{2y+1}}{2})(2y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{({y}^{2}+4){e}^{2y+1}}{2}-\\int y\\,{e}^{2y+1}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int y\\,{e}^{2y+1}\\,\\text{d}y.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=y\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}={e}^{2y+1}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y=\\int {e}^{2y+1}\\,\\text{d}y=\\frac{{e}^{2y+1}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y\\,{e}^{2y+1}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =(y)(\\frac{{e}^{2y+1}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{2y+1}}{2})(1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{y\\,{e}^{2y+1}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2y+1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{y\\,{e}^{2y+1}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2y+1}}{4}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({y}^{2}+4){e}^{2y+1}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\frac{({y}^{2}+4){e}^{2y+1}}{2}-\\int y\\,{e}^{2y+1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{({y}^{2}+4){e}^{2y+1}}{2}-(\\frac{y\\,{e}^{2y+1}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2y+1}}{4}+D) \\\\  & =\\frac{({y}^{2}+4){e}^{2y+1}}{2}-\\frac{y\\,{e}^{2y+1}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{2y+1}}{4}-D \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2y+1}}{4}(2{y}^{2}-2y+9)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-D.$ \n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2}+4){e}^{2y+1}\\,\\text{d}y & ={[\\frac{{e}^{2y+1}}{4}(2{y}^{2}-2y+9)]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{e}^{2(2)+1}}{4}(2(2)^{2}-2(2)+9))-(\\frac{{e}^{2(0)+1}}{4}(2(0)^{2}-2(0)+9)) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{5}}{4}(8-4+9)-\\frac{e}{4}(0-0+9) \\\\  & =\\frac{13{e}^{5}}{4}-\\frac{9e}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{13{e}^{5}-9e}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320945 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98a67346-c2c9-5d22-8adf-479fea81eaa2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of a Logarithmic Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "744e8198-302b-5b3f-865b-77ee955ad8cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "744e8198-302b-5b3f-865b-77ee955ad8cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319741} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d5038e6-e447-5d80-9e3f-bde4a002b130" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320392}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c1d37f39-dd10-54e5-8b85-a29e10f1a31c" :question/math-academy-id "q-352040" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1/3}^{e/3}3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2} & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=3 & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 3\\,\\text{d}x=3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}(3x)-\\int (3x)(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}-\\int 6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int 6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=6 & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 6\\,\\text{d}x=6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)(6x)-\\int (6x)(\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)-\\int 6\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)-6x+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =3x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}-\\int 6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}-(6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)-6x+D) \\\\  & =3x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}-6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)+6x-D \\\\  & =3x((\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)+2)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-D.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/3}^{e/3}3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}\\text{d}x & ={[3x((\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)+2)]}_{1/3}^{e/3} \\\\  & =[3(\\frac{e}{3})({(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{e}{3}))}^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{e}{3})+2)]-[3(\\frac{1}{3})({(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}))}^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3})+2)] \\\\  & =[e((\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+2)]-[1((\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+2)] \\\\  & =[e(1-2+2)]-[1(0-0+2)] \\\\  & =e-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320947 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98a67346-c2c9-5d22-8adf-479fea81eaa2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of a Logarithmic Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14560af1-da74-5f36-a18b-b748d25b48a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{e}^{3}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14560af1-da74-5f36-a18b-b748d25b48a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{e}^{3}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319448} :answer-field/id #uuid "b77a2913-0af3-5bc9-8b65-91a3fcf636d6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320531}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4d3bd2be-c388-5990-aa44-fdfa96c596f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-352056" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{e-1}27(x+1)^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))^{2} & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}{x+1}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=27(x+1)^{2} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 27(x+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=9(x+1)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 27(x+1)^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))}^{2}(9(x+1)^{3})-\\int (9(x+1)^{3})(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}{x+1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =9(x+1)^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))}^{2}-18\\int (x+1)^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int (x+1)^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1) & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=(x+1)^{2} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int (x+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{(x+1)^{3}}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (x+1)^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)(\\frac{(x+1)^{3}}{3})-\\int (\\frac{(x+1)^{3}}{3})(\\frac{1}{x+1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\int (x+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}{3}-\\frac{(x+1)^{3}}{9}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 27(x+1)^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =9(x+1)^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))}^{2}-18\\int (x+1)^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =9(x+1)^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))}^{2}-18(\\frac{(x+1)^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}{3}-\\frac{(x+1)^{3}}{9}+D) \\\\  & =9(x+1)^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))}^{2}-6(x+1)^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+2(x+1)^{3}-18D \\\\  & =(x+1)^{3}(9{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))}^{2}-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+2)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-18D.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{e-1}27(x+1)^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))^{2}\\text{d}x & ={[(x+1)^{3}(9{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))}^{2}-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+2)]}_{0}^{e-1} \\\\  & =[{e}^{3}(9{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)}^{2}-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+2)]-[{1}^{3}(9{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)}^{2}-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+2)] \\\\  & =[{e}^{3}(9-6+2)]-2 \\\\  & =5{e}^{3}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547046 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b69f026a-50b3-505d-bd9d-8b295691b66e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of an Exponential Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25a922f4-2d9d-5871-8561-90a11f1180f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546335} {:answer/id #uuid "333ff7b8-358e-5ce3-b123-bd1e014088ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546363} {:answer/id #uuid "5faa619c-acfc-59d4-95ba-cf127af6cc6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546457} {:answer/id #uuid "9a52084e-204b-5a20-b7fa-46763ae8012e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546597} {:answer/id #uuid "dea2d307-2cdd-5b5e-97e7-7d1ce5fb5e94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5faa619c-acfc-59d4-95ba-cf127af6cc6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546457} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3718fae-c042-5c2d-a11b-debd358108e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b7ebc1ef-47dd-5be6-aa58-7b0c8c511acd" :question/math-academy-id "q-49929" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1}4({z}^{2}+1){e}^{2z}\\,\\text{d}z.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={z}^{2}+1\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}=2z, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}z}=4{e}^{2z}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}z}\\,\\text{d}z=4\\int {e}^{2z}\\,\\text{d}z=2{e}^{2z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4({z}^{2}+1){e}^{2z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =({z}^{2}+1)(2{e}^{2z})-\\int (2{e}^{2z})(2z)\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2({z}^{2}+1){e}^{2z}-4\\int z{e}^{2z}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int z{e}^{2z}\\text{d}z.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=z\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}z}={e}^{2z}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}z}\\,\\text{d}z=\\int {e}^{2z}\\,\\text{d}z=\\frac{{e}^{2z}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int z{e}^{2z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =(z)(\\frac{{e}^{2z}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{2z}}{2})(1)\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\frac{z{e}^{2z}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\frac{z{e}^{2z}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2z}}{4}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4({z}^{2}+1){e}^{2z}\\,\\text{d}z & =2({z}^{2}+1){e}^{2z}-4\\int z{e}^{2z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2({z}^{2}+1){e}^{2z}-4(\\frac{z{e}^{2z}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2z}}{4}+D) \\\\  & =2({z}^{2}+1){e}^{2z}-2z{e}^{2z}+{e}^{2z}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-4D.$ \n\nFinally, then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4({z}^{2}+1){e}^{2z}\\,\\text{d}z & =(2({z}^{2}+1){e}^{2z}-2z{e}^{2z}+{e}^{2z}){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(2({1}^{2}+1){(e)}^{2}-2(1)^{2}{e}^{2}+{e}^{2})-(2(0+1){e}^{0}-2\\cdot 0\\cdot {e}^{0}+{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{2}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332813 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2a41ede-381e-55f6-99e5-1baf1ea348a1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of a Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2049d209-f71d-5036-a5ed-1c707b8cca2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{3}{8}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330227} {:answer/id #uuid "2a1e5edf-0eb9-5389-987f-0ab7fb0a9f0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)}{3}+\\frac{5}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330322} {:answer/id #uuid "498eeb73-48b5-5b64-8c4a-7b75c33fa56e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330609} {:answer/id #uuid "abb3132c-a6e4-5d7f-896f-2f57e4856624" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+1)}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331473} {:answer/id #uuid "b1273dac-06c9-51ed-931a-5a5528347a85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331517}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "498eeb73-48b5-5b64-8c4a-7b75c33fa56e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330609} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7be81ae-8223-53d0-8023-701f2e8a7fd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332607}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2e8ec66f-4e06-54fb-9f1e-056dedad6cc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-50226" :question/problem "$\\int (4{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. \n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=4{x}^{2}+1\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(4{x}^{2}+1)(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})-\\int (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547073 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98a67346-c2c9-5d22-8adf-479fea81eaa2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of a Logarithmic Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16b56f03-d3ba-5b8a-beda-1ede1217e4eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{27}(3{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2})}^{2}-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}+5)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546301} {:answer/id #uuid "29523a5f-ea36-5d82-b658-db73edef63bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{27}(9{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2})}^{2}-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}+8)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546341} {:answer/id #uuid "4c335eff-2b6b-524d-90cf-5442e47c7a6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{9}(3{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2})}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}+2)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546421} {:answer/id #uuid "54e90af3-fa72-54c7-92dc-5200072bed0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{9}({(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2})}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}-4)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546438} {:answer/id #uuid "b864fa64-c785-5bce-9f26-627200acd91a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{27}(6{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2})}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546664}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29523a5f-ea36-5d82-b658-db73edef63bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{27}(9{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2})}^{2}-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}+8)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546341} :answer-field/id #uuid "4681467c-286a-5fcb-8e52-ae9fe0e595eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546856}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eec4d3ca-fb39-5950-ae42-a7772eee1331" :question/math-academy-id "q-50230" :question/problem "$\\int 4{x}^{2}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2})}^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2})}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\cdot 2x=\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=4{x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 4{x}^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4{x}^{2}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2})}^{2}(\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3})-\\int (\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3})(\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4{x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2})}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{16}{3}\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}))(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})-\\int (\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})(\\frac{2}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})}{3}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{9}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4{x}^{2}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{4{x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2})}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{16}{3}(\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})}{3}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{9}+D) \\\\  & =\\frac{4{x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2})}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{16{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})}{9}+\\frac{32{x}^{3}}{27}-\\frac{16D}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{27}(9{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2})}^{2}-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}+8)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhere, $C=-\\frac{16D}{3}.$"}]}
